        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best low-cost treadmill for running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              The Horizon T101 Connect, at about $777 on Amazon on 21 November 2026. It has a 20 by 55-inch running area, 10 mph, 10% power incline and a lifetime frame and motor warranty. If you need to spend nearer $600, Sunny’s Auto Incline Treadmill gives a 20 by 53-inch surface and 10 mph.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best running machine under $500?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              There is no true running treadmill under $500 in our view, because nothing we found at that price pairs a 20-inch belt with a length of 50 inches or more. The cheapest machine that reaches 10 mph is the ATEEDGE at about $370, but its 18.5 by 45.5-inch belt makes it a jogging machine. The BORGUSI at about $570 is the cheapest with a 20-inch belt.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the best treadmill for runners on a budget?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most runners, the Horizon T101 Connect or the NordicTrack T 6.5 S, both with a 20 by 55-inch belt and 10 mph. The Horizon has the stronger motor warranty (lifetime); the NordicTrack is about $160 cheaper and works with iFIT. Runners over about 5 foot 10 should save for a 60-inch deck instead.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you run on a cheap treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              You can jog on many of them, but most cheap treadmills are built for walking. Check three things before you buy: a belt at least 20 inches wide and 50 to 55 inches long, a top speed of 10 mph, and a motor warranty stated in writing. The word “running” in a listing title is not a specification.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What top speed do I need on a treadmill for running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Ten mph, a 6-minute mile, covers almost every home runner, including most interval work. An 8 mph ceiling is a 7:30 mile, fine for easy running but limiting for faster repeats. Only choose a 12 mph machine if your fastest intervals are quicker than 6:00 per mile.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is 3.0 HP enough for running on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is if it is 3.0 continuous horsepower (CHP), which is the figure our horsepower guide suggests for running. Most budget listings do not say whether their figure is continuous or peak, and a peak figure can be much higher than what the motor sustains. Without a CHP rating, judge the motor by its warranty.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a walking pad good for running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Most walking pads top out at 4 mph, and 2-in-1 pads with a handle reach about 6 to 7.5 mph on belts around 40 to 50 inches long and 16 to 17 inches wide. That suits walking and a light jog. For running, start at the 20-inch belts on this page.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

